data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?**\n\n**The short version:** Carts have outgrown the 4 KB session cookie, so they need to live on the server. The question is which server-side store to use.\n\n**Example:** A shopper adds 30 items across several sessions. Their cart is too big to fit in the browser cookie, so the cart has to be saved on the server and the browser keeps only an ID that points to it.\n\n**Option a1: Redis**\n- Very fast, and built for short-lived data like sessions.\n- Carts can expire on their own (for example, after 30 days of inactivity).\n- Downsides: it's another service to run and monitor. Unless persistence is configured, a restart or memory eviction can wipe carts, and shoppers would lose items.\n- Reporting on carts (like \"how many abandoned carts had shoes?\") is awkward.\n\n**Option a2: Postgres (the main agent's recommendation)**\n- Carts survive restarts, and the database is likely already in use, so there's no new service.\n- Writes are a little slower than Redis, but that's fine at typical cart traffic.\n- Carts can be queried and reported on, and cart changes can share a transaction with order creation.\n- Expiry needs a scheduled cleanup job instead of a built-in TTL.\n\n**My recommendation: a2 (Postgres).** Carts are data you don't want to lose, and Postgres gives durability and reporting without adding infrastructure. Pick Redis only if you already run it for other things and the speed matters more than durability.\n\n**Caveat:** The main checkout at `project/` has no files, only the `init` commit, so I couldn't check the existing stack. This explanation is based on the ask text alone. If the repo already uses Postgres or Redis, that should weigh heavily toward the option already in use.\n\nA1 is still open. Choose a1 or a2 in the Asks panel, or tell me here and I'll resolve it with your choice and a note for the main agent."}}

